course: dictionary
lesson: 13413
title: Hierarchy: levels of importance
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A hierarchy is a way of arranging things in levels, with the most important or powerful at the top and the least important at the bottom. «The school has a hierarchy: the head teacher is at the top, then the deputies, then the teachers.» The words top and levels are the clues. You can picture it as a ladder or a pyramid.
explain: Look for clues around the word. «In the castle, the king was at the top of the hierarchy, and the peasants worked in the fields at the bottom.» The words top, bottom and who was more powerful all tell you what hierarchy means. Ask yourself: is something being ranked from high to low? If it is, hierarchy is probably the right word.
explain: The word can also mean the people in charge of an organisation, such as a church. «The hierarchy of the church met to choose a new leader.» Here it means the bishops, priests and deacons who run the church. Now try your meaning in the sentence: does «the people in charge met» make sense? It does, so you are on the right track.
fact: A hierarchy is a system where things are arranged in levels.
fact: «Hierarchy» can mean the people in charge of an organisation, such as a church.
fact: In science, living things are sorted in a hierarchy: kingdom, class, order, family, genus, species.
fact: In maths, the order of operations works like a hierarchy, with some calculations done before others.
fact: Phrases to know: «at the top of the hierarchy», «social hierarchy», and «a pecking order», which means the same kind of ranking.
exercise: «In the old castle, the king was at the top of the hierarchy.» What is at the top of a hierarchy? | *The most important or powerful | The place where things get lost | The middle, always | The smallest and least important
exercise: «The team has a clear hierarchy: the captain leads, then the vice-captain, then the players.» What does hierarchy mean here? | Players who never train | *Levels, from the most important at the top to the least at the bottom | A type of football boot | A large, flat team with no leader
exercise: Priests, bishops and deacons all work together in church life. What do they form? | A puzzle | A swamp | A recipe | *A hierarchy
test: «The wolf pack has a hierarchy, with the strongest wolf at the top and the youngest at the bottom.» What does the sentence tell you about hierarchy? | Wolves do not live in groups | Hierarchy means hunting at night | Hierarchy means a type of forest | *Animals can be arranged in levels, the strongest at the top
test: Kingdom, class, order, family, genus and species are the levels used to sort living things. What is this called? | A dance | *A hierarchy | Rules for football | A list of countries
exam: «Ella said the class had a pecking order, but her teacher called it a hierarchy.» What do the two words have in common? | *They both mean things ranked in levels | They both mean a type of food | They mean the same as the word lesson | They both mean a sport played outdoors
